<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
          http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
          https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.23.xsd">

    <!--
      "Povezivanje bez obavezne prijave" — BLOK-LINK.md §7.1.

      Signing in used to be what told the server WHO may write a score into a
      linked table. §7 removes that requirement, so the same question needs a
      different answer, and it is the one this project already uses for staff
      who have no account (`tournament_waiter_sessions.token`): a per-link
      bearer secret, minted once by `services/ClaimTokens` and returned to the
      client exactly once, on creation.

      Two changes, and they only make sense together:

        * `write_token varchar(48)` — UNIQUE so a token names at most one
          link, NULLABLE because every link created before §7 has none and
          must keep working through its requester's uid. 48 is the width the
          other ClaimTokens columns use (`pairs.claim_token`); the generator
          emits 32 characters, and the slack is for a future widening rather
          than for anything stored today.

        * `requested_by_uid` LOSES its NOT NULL. An anonymous requester has no
          Firebase uid to store — that is the entire point of §7 — and the
          column is the natural place for "there was none". Dropping the
          constraint is what makes an anonymous row insertable at all, so
          this is not a separate concern that could be deferred: without it
          POST /blok-links would still be closed to anonymous callers, just
          with a 500 instead of a 401.

      What did NOT change, and must not: the organiser's approval is still the
      only real defence (BLOK-LINK.md §7.1, last line). A write token proves
      "I am the device that asked for THIS link" and nothing else — it cannot
      approve itself, cannot reach another link, and is useless until an
      organiser has moved the row to APPROVED.

      No backfill and no index beyond the unique constraint: the token is only
      ever looked up as "does the one link I already loaded by uuid carry
      this?", never as a lookup key of its own, so the constraint is there to
      keep two links from colliding rather than to serve a query.
    -->

    <changeSet id="2026-09-08-blok-link-write-token" author="mrodek">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="match_score_links" columnName="write_token"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <addColumn tableName="match_score_links">
            <!-- The device secret handed out once, at creation. NULL on every
                 link made before §7, and on any link whose owner never needed
                 one. -->
            <column name="write_token" type="varchar(48)">
                <constraints unique="true" uniqueConstraintName="uq_msl_write_token"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>

        <!-- An anonymous requester has no uid. See the note above. -->
        <dropNotNullConstraint tableName="match_score_links"
                               columnName="requested_by_uid"
                               columnDataType="varchar(64)"/>

        <rollback>
            <addNotNullConstraint tableName="match_score_links"
                                  columnName="requested_by_uid"
                                  columnDataType="varchar(64)"/>
            <dropColumn tableName="match_score_links" columnName="write_token"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
